# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Domain 6 additions
_xfc('sscpNetwork', _L7, [
    ("Network topologies (bus, star, ring, mesh)",
     "Topology is the layout of nodes and links. A bus shares one cable, a ring passes data around a loop, a star connects every node to a central switch, and a mesh links nodes directly to several others. A full mesh of n nodes needs n(n-1)/2 links.",
     "Star is the usual LAN design, and its central switch is a single point of failure. Mesh gives the best redundancy at the highest cost, so partial mesh is common in WANs."),
    ("Peer-to-peer vs. client-server relationships",
     "In client-server networking, central servers provide services and enforce access control, logging and updates to clients. In peer-to-peer networking, each node can act as both client and server, with no central control.",
     "Client-server is easier to secure and monitor because policy is central. Peer-to-peer file sharing on corporate networks causes data leakage and malware risk and is normally blocked."),
    ("Transmission media: copper, fiber and wireless",
     "Twisted-pair copper is cheap, limited to about 100 metres for Ethernet and prone to electromagnetic interference and tapping. Fiber carries light over long distances, is immune to electromagnetic interference and is harder to tap. Wireless is convenient but exposed to anyone in range.",
     "Single-mode fiber covers long distances and multimode shorter runs. Use fiber near heavy electrical noise or between buildings, and encrypt wireless traffic."),
    ("Software-defined networking (SDN)",
     "SDN separates the control plane, which decides how traffic flows, from the data plane, which forwards it. A central controller programs the switches through APIs such as OpenFlow, which enables automation, rapid segmentation and consistent policy.",
     "The controller and its APIs become high-value targets, so protect them with strong authentication, segmentation and change control. A bad central change can break everything at once."),
    ("Software-defined WAN (SD-WAN)",
     "SD-WAN manages connections between sites over several transports, such as broadband, MPLS and cellular, using central policy to steer applications over the best path and normally encrypting traffic with IPsec tunnels.",
     "It reduces cost and improves resilience, but routing traffic straight to the internet from branches means security services such as firewall and web filtering must move to the branch or the cloud, often as SASE."),
    ("Network virtualization and automation",
     "Virtual switches, overlays such as VXLAN and virtual firewalls let networks be built in software, and orchestration tools and infrastructure as code configure them automatically from templates.",
     "Automation applies a mistake everywhere very quickly. Keep templates under version control, review and test changes, and watch for drift from the approved configuration."),
    ("Well-known ports worth memorizing",
     "FTP 20 and 21, SSH and SFTP 22, Telnet 23, SMTP 25, DNS 53, DHCP 67 and 68, HTTP 80, Kerberos 88, POP3 110, NTP 123, IMAP 143, SNMP 161, LDAP 389, HTTPS 443, SMB 445, LDAPS 636, IMAPS 993, POP3S 995 and RDP 3389.",
     "Pair each cleartext port with its secure replacement. TACACS+ uses TCP 49, and RADIUS uses UDP 1812 and 1813. Exposing RDP, SMB or Telnet to the internet is a serious finding."),
    ("DNS cache poisoning",
     "An attacker slips a forged answer into a resolver's cache so that a legitimate name resolves to an address the attacker controls, sending users to a fake site. Defences include DNSSEC validation, randomized source ports and transaction IDs, patched resolvers and restricting who may use recursion.",
     "Users see the correct name in the address bar yet reach the wrong server. DNSSEC lets resolvers detect forged records but does not encrypt queries."),
    ("Amplification and reflection attacks",
     "The attacker sends small requests with the victim's address spoofed as the source to servers such as open DNS resolvers or NTP servers, which return much larger replies to the victim. Defences are ingress and egress filtering, closing open resolvers, rate limiting and upstream scrubbing.",
     "UDP services are the usual abuse because they have no handshake. The victim cannot easily block the sources because they are legitimate third-party servers."),
    ("Content delivery networks and DDoS protection",
     "A content delivery network serves content from many distributed edge locations, which improves performance and absorbs volumetric DDoS traffic. DDoS protection services scrub attack traffic upstream, and many add a web application firewall.",
     "Hide the origin server's address so attackers cannot bypass the CDN. Capacity planning cannot beat a large botnet, so use an upstream service."),
    ("Thin clients and virtual desktop infrastructure (VDI)",
     "A thin client has little local storage and displays a desktop that runs on a central server. Virtual desktop infrastructure hosts these desktops in a data center, so data stays in the data center and clipboard, drive and print redirection can be restricted.",
     "Good for contractors and unmanaged devices, but the hypervisor, image and gateway need protection, and a compromised endpoint can still capture keystrokes and screens."),
    ("Remote access operation and configuration",
     "Remote access uses a VPN, a remote desktop gateway, a jump host or zero trust network access, always with strong authentication, MFA and logging. Configure least privilege, session timeouts and device posture checks, and do not expose services such as RDP directly to the internet.",
     "Publicly exposed remote desktop is a leading ransomware entry point. Record privileged remote sessions."),
    ("Data plane, control plane and management plane",
     "On a router or switch, the data plane forwards packets, the control plane builds the forwarding tables through protocols such as OSPF and BGP, and the management plane is how administrators configure it, through SSH, SNMP or a console.",
     "Protect each plane differently: authenticate routing protocols and limit control-plane traffic, and keep management on a dedicated network with AAA."),
    ("Logical and physical placement of network devices",
     "Inline devices such as firewalls and IPS sit in the traffic path and can block, but a failure can stop traffic. Passive devices such as IDS sensors read a copy from a tap or span port and cannot block. Virtual appliances run on hypervisors to inspect traffic between workloads.",
     "Place inspection where the traffic of interest actually flows. East-west traffic inside a virtual environment never reaches a perimeter firewall."),
    ("Out-of-band management network",
     "A separate management network, with its own switches or console servers, is used to administer devices even when the production network is down or compromised. Access is limited to administrators through a jump host with MFA and logging.",
     "In-band management over the production network is simpler but exposes management interfaces to everyone on it. Out-of-band also lets you fix a device that has lost its network."),
    ("Physical vs. logical segmentation and firewall zones",
     "Physical segmentation uses separate hardware or air gaps, and logical segmentation uses VLANs, subnets and firewall rules. Firewall zones group networks by trust, such as untrusted, DMZ, internal and restricted, with policy controlling traffic between zones.",
     "Physical separation is stronger but costly and less flexible. Even with VLANs, inter-zone traffic must pass through a filtering device."),
    ("Web application firewall (WAF)",
     "A firewall that inspects HTTP and HTTPS at the application layer, blocking attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting using rules and learned behaviour. It is deployed as a reverse proxy or a cloud service in front of web applications.",
     "A WAF can virtually patch a flaw while code is fixed, but it does not replace secure coding. Ordinary network firewalls do not read web requests."),
    ("Load balancers",
     "A load balancer spreads requests across servers, checks their health and removes failed ones, improving availability and scale. Layer 7 balancers can route by content and offload TLS, and they can also hide server addresses.",
     "A load balancer is itself a single point of failure unless it is paired. It also terminates TLS, so protect its keys."),
    ("Traffic shaping and WAN optimization",
     "Traffic shaping uses quality of service rules to prioritize critical traffic such as voice and to limit less important flows. WAN optimization reduces bandwidth with compression, deduplication and caching.",
     "Shaping can limit abusive traffic during a flood. Encrypted traffic is harder to optimize and classify unless the devices are trusted endpoints of the connection."),
    ("Unified threat management (UTM)",
     "An all-in-one appliance that combines firewalling, intrusion prevention, antivirus, web filtering, VPN and often spam filtering in one product with central management. It suits small sites that want simple, lower-cost protection.",
     "The trade-off is a single point of failure and shared performance when features are all enabled. Large organizations usually use specialized layers."),
    ("Router and switch security",
     "Harden routers with access control lists, authenticated routing protocols and disabled unneeded services. Harden switches with port security, DHCP snooping, dynamic ARP inspection, BPDU guard and by shutting unused ports and keeping them out of VLAN 1.",
     "Layer 2 attacks such as ARP spoofing and rogue DHCP need local access, so access ports are the place to defend."),
    ("Subnetting and CIDR notation",
     "CIDR notation gives the prefix length, so a /24 has 256 addresses and 254 usable hosts, a /26 has 64 addresses and 62 usable hosts, and a /30 has 4 addresses and 2 usable hosts. Smaller subnets limit broadcast domains and make segmentation rules precise.",
     "Usable hosts equal 2 to the power of host bits minus 2, for the network and broadcast addresses. A /32 identifies a single host."),
    ("Cellular network security",
     "Cellular networks (4G LTE and 5G) provide wide-area connectivity with encryption between the device and the carrier, not end to end. Risks include SIM swapping, fake base stations that capture subscriber identity and traffic, and SMS interception. 5G improves protection of the subscriber's permanent identifier.",
     "Treat the cellular path as untrusted for confidentiality and use a VPN or end-to-end encryption. SMS is a weak second factor because of SIM swapping."),
    ("Bluetooth security",
     "Short-range wireless technology with typical range of around 10 metres. Attacks include bluejacking (unsolicited messages), bluesnarfing (stealing data) and bluebugging (taking control). Defences are turning Bluetooth off when unused, non-discoverable mode, secure pairing and patching.",
     "Pairing in a crowded place and leaving devices discoverable expose them. Updates matter because many Bluetooth flaws are in the stack."),
    ("Near-field communication (NFC)",
     "A very short range wireless technology, normally a few centimetres, used for contactless payment, badges and tap-to-pair. Risks include eavesdropping within range, relay attacks and skimming. Mobile payments use tokenization so the real card number is not sent.",
     "Short range limits exposure but does not remove it. Keep the device locked and disable NFC if it is not needed."),
    ("Wi-Fi Protected Access evolution (WEP, WPA, WPA2, WPA3)",
     "WEP uses RC4 and is broken. WPA used TKIP as an interim fix. WPA2 uses AES-CCMP. WPA3 adds Simultaneous Authentication of Equals for personal networks, mandatory protected management frames, an optional 192-bit mode for enterprise, and Opportunistic Wireless Encryption for open networks.",
     "WPA2 Personal with a weak passphrase can be attacked offline, which WPA3 SAE prevents. Choose WPA3 Enterprise when per-user identity is needed."),
    ("Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) methods",
     "EAP is a framework for authentication used with 802.1X. EAP-TLS uses certificates on both the server and the client and is the strongest. PEAP and EAP-TTLS protect an inner password exchange in a tunnel created with a server certificate.",
     "Always validate the server certificate on the client, or a rogue access point can harvest credentials. LEAP is obsolete."),
    ("IoT configuration and network isolation",
     "Internet of Things devices should have default credentials changed, unneeded services disabled and management restricted, and should live on a separate network segment with firewall rules that allow only the traffic they need.",
     "Many IoT devices cannot run agents or be patched quickly, so isolation and monitoring of their traffic are the main controls."),
    ("IoT firmware updates and end-of-life management",
     "Choose devices with signed, supported firmware updates and a published support period, patch them on a schedule, and plan replacement before the vendor ends support. Devices that cannot be updated are isolated or retired.",
     "An unsupported camera or sensor on the network is a permanent vulnerability. Record device owners, firmware versions and support dates in the inventory."),
])

